    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [3, 1, 2, 4]  ->  [4, 2, 1, 3]   (also accepted: [2, 4, 3, 1], [4, 2, 3, 1], ...)\nnums = [0]           ->  [0]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: partition, as quick sort does"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This isn't a full sort — values only need to land in the right **group**. That's exactly the partition step of quick sort, with \"is it even?\" in place of \"is it below the pivot?\". Keep two pointers: `i` moves right from the front past values that are already even, `j` moves left from the back past values that are already odd. When both stop, `nums[i]` is odd and `nums[j]` is even — each belongs on the other side — so swap them. When the pointers meet, every value is in its half."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Two passes into a new array"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function sortArrayByParity(nums) {\n  const out = [];\n  for (const x of nums) if (x % 2 === 0) out.push(x);   // evens first\n  for (const x of nums) if (x % 2 === 1) out.push(x);   // then odds\n  return out;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def sort_array_by_parity(nums):\n    return [x for x in nums if x % 2 == 0] + [x for x in nums if x % 2 == 1]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] sortArrayByParity(int[] nums) {\n    int[] out = new int[nums.length];\n    int k = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) if (x % 2 == 0) out[k++] = x;   // evens first\n    for (int x : nums) if (x % 2 == 1) out[k++] = x;   // then odds\n    return out;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> sortArrayByParity(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> out;\n    for (int x : nums) if (x % 2 == 0) out.push_back(x);   // evens first\n    for (int x : nums) if (x % 2 == 1) out.push_back(x);   // then odds\n    return out;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n).** Simple, and it keeps the original order within each group (it's stable)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. In place with two pointers"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "sort-by-parity",
      "caption": "i skips evens at the front, j skips odds at the back; when both are stuck, swap."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function sortArrayByParity(nums) {\n  let i = 0, j = nums.length - 1;\n  while (i < j) {\n    if (nums[i] % 2 === 0) i++;                     // already an even at the front\n    else if (nums[j] % 2 === 1) j--;                // already an odd at the back\n    else {\n      [nums[i], nums[j]] = [nums[j], nums[i]];      // odd at the front, even at the back: swap\n      i++;\n      j--;\n    }\n  }\n  return nums;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def sort_array_by_parity(nums):\n    i, j = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while i < j:\n        if nums[i] % 2 == 0:\n            i += 1                        # already an even at the front\n        elif nums[j] % 2 == 1:\n            j -= 1                        # already an odd at the back\n        else:\n            nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]   # odd at the front, even at the back: swap\n            i += 1\n            j -= 1\n    return nums"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] sortArrayByParity(int[] nums) {\n    int i = 0, j = nums.length - 1;\n    while (i < j) {\n        if (nums[i] % 2 == 0) i++;                   // already an even at the front\n        else if (nums[j] % 2 == 1) j--;              // already an odd at the back\n        else {\n            int t = nums[i];                         // odd at the front, even at the back: swap\n            nums[i] = nums[j];\n            nums[j] = t;\n            i++;\n            j--;\n        }\n    }\n    return nums;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> sortArrayByParity(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int i = 0, j = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n    while (i < j) {\n        if (nums[i] % 2 == 0) i++;                   // already an even at the front\n        else if (nums[j] % 2 == 1) j--;              // already an odd at the back\n        else swap(nums[i++], nums[j--]);             // odd at the front, even at the back: swap\n    }\n    return nums;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** Each step moves at least one pointer, so there are at most n steps. Unlike approach 1 it doesn't keep the original order inside the groups — the question doesn't ask it to."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Two passes into a new array",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "**Two passes**: evens first, then odds.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **new array**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "In place with two pointers",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "Each step moves **at least one pointer**: at most n steps.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only **two pointers**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**All even or all odd** — one pointer runs to the other; nothing moves.",
        "**One value** — returned as it is.",
        "**Zero** — even.",
        "**Negative numbers** (in variants) — in Java and C++, `-3 % 2` is `-1`: test oddness with `x % 2 != 0`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Sort Array By Parity II** — evens at even indexes and odds at odd ones: two pointers that each jump by 2.",
        "**Sort Colors** — three groups: the Dutch national flag partition (Arrays course).",
        "**Move Zeroes** — a partition that must keep the non-zero values in order (Arrays course)."
      ]
    }
  ],
